% ============================================================================
% seed-dispersal-mechanism — four named ways a plant disperses its seeds and
% how each actually works (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A plant cannot walk its own seeds away from itself, so it relies on one of
% a small set of physical mechanisms to move them: gravity simply lets them
% fall, an exploding fruit ejects them, wind carries them, and an animal's
% fur carries them stuck to its outside. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? seed_dispersal_mechanism(barochory, $D)
%       % uses_gravity_as_a_simple_means_of_seed_dispersal
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `seed_dispersal_mechanism(mechanism, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a seed-dispersal-related term that is not one
% of these four. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and
% recall the mechanism it names:
%
%     ? seed_dispersal_mechanism($M, seed_is_forcefully_ejected_by_explosive_dehiscence_of_the_fruit)
%       % ballochory
%
% The four mechanisms, as a little truth table:
%
%     mechanism      description                                                        quote (verbatim)
%     -------------  -----------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     barochory       uses_gravity_as_a_simple_means_of_seed_dispersal                     "Barochory or the plant use of gravity for dispersal is a simple means of achieving seed dispersal."
%     ballochory      seed_is_forcefully_ejected_by_explosive_dehiscence_of_the_fruit       "Ballochory is a type of dispersal where the seed is forcefully ejected by explosive dehiscence of the fruit."
%     anemochory      seeds_float_on_the_breeze_or_flutter_to_the_ground                    "Wind dispersal (anemochory) is one of the more primitive means of dispersal. Wind dispersal can take on one of two primary forms: seeds or fruits can float on the breeze or, alternatively, they can flutter to the ground."
%     epizoochory     transported_on_the_outside_of_vertebrate_animals                      "Seeds can be transported on the outside of vertebrate animals (mostly mammals), a process known as epizoochory."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Wikipedia's "Seed dispersal" article,
% which gives each of these four mechanisms a defining sentence in its own
% subsection -- except that anemochory needs two (see below), and
% epizoochory's sits in the shared "Animals: epi- and endozoochory"
% subsection. This is a MULTI-SOURCE-STYLE table
% (see `ocean-current-drivers.adj`) in the sense that each row's quote
% comes from a different subsection of the same page rather than a single
% shared sentence -- each row quotes its own subsection.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the barochory quote, so a ballochory, anemochory or
% epizoochory answer was warranted primarily by a sentence about gravity
% dispersal, and the other three quotes reached no answer at all. Each row
% now carries its own subsection's sentence, and the envelope is the
% article's framing sentence, which names no mechanism.
%
% THE ANEMOCHORY ROW CARRIES TWO SENTENCES. Its quote never says
% "anemochory" -- it opens "Wind dispersal can take on one of two primary
% forms" -- so read detached from the page it grounds nothing. The
% sentence before it in the same paragraph does name the mechanism, and
% the two are contiguous, so the row takes both. That is the remedy
% ../README.md prescribes under "A citation must name its own subject":
% widen the quote until it is self-contained.
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the
% same host returns 404 and holds none of these spans), tags removed
% without a space and only ASCII whitespace collapsed: each row's span and
% the envelope each occur exactly once, each inside one `<p>`, scripts set
% aside and over the whole file. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no
% row restates a `locator` line or a `trust` tier of its own.
%
% `trust consensus` — Wikipedia, the same tier this stdlib already reserves for
% other Wikipedia citations (e.g. `pond-zone.adj`).
%
% Two real mechanisms the SAME article also names are deliberately NOT
% rows here, so a recall ABSTAINS on them rather than inventing a
% description:
%   - `hydrochory` (water dispersal): the page's Water section opens "Many
%     aquatic (water dwelling) and some terrestrial (land dwelling) species
%     use hydrochory, or seed dispersal through water." That names the
%     mechanism, but its definition is an APPOSITIVE inside a sentence about
%     WHICH SPECIES use it, not a sentence stating what the mechanism does;
%     the section's next sentence redirects to dispersal DISTANCE. So the
%     row would quote a definition that is grammatically a subordinate
%     clause of a different claim.
%   - `endozoochory` (dispersal by ingestion and defecation): its own
%     defining sentence bundles the definition together with a separate,
%     distinct empirical claim ("...is the dispersal mechanism for most
%     tree species") rather than stating one clean fact the way the four
%     tabled here do.
% This slice separately confirmed via three additional non-Wikipedia
% sources (a U.S. National Park Service page that 404'd, USDA Forest
% Service research papers that describe seed dispersal via multi-fact
% academic prose rather than clean single-sentence definitions, and a
% kids'-science page whose per-mechanism descriptions are each bundled
% with worked examples) that this topic resists the "extend an existing
% table" and "single clean kids'-education source" patterns this stdlib
% has otherwise relied on — Wikipedia's own per-mechanism subsections were
% the only source found with genuinely standalone defining sentences.
% Picked using the mandatory full-tree-grep-before-scoping discipline —
% zero hits for
% "barochory"/"ballochory"/"anemochory"/"epizoochory"/"seed_dispersal"
% before this file was written. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table seed_dispersal_mechanism {
    columns mechanism, description

    row (barochory, uses_gravity_as_a_simple_means_of_seed_dispersal) {
        source "Barochory or the plant use of gravity for dispersal is a simple means of achieving seed dispersal."
    }
    row (ballochory, seed_is_forcefully_ejected_by_explosive_dehiscence_of_the_fruit) {
        source "Ballochory is a type of dispersal where the seed is forcefully ejected by explosive dehiscence of the fruit."
    }
    row (anemochory, seeds_float_on_the_breeze_or_flutter_to_the_ground) {
        source "Wind dispersal (anemochory) is one of the more primitive means of dispersal. Wind dispersal can take on one of two primary forms: seeds or fruits can float on the breeze or, alternatively, they can flutter to the ground."
    }
    row (epizoochory, transported_on_the_outside_of_vertebrate_animals) {
        source "Seeds can be transported on the outside of vertebrate animals (mostly mammals), a process known as epizoochory."
    }

    source "Seeds can be dispersed away from the parent plant individually or collectively, as well as dispersed in both space and time."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seed_dispersal"
    trust consensus
}
